      "dns_teaching": {
        "prerequisite_note": "Development check: explains the editor before exercising all supported record types. This is not a finished authored lesson.",
        "concepts": [
          {
            "title": "Names point to data",
            "text": "A maps a full name to an IPv4 address; AAAA maps it to an IPv6 address. CNAME points to another full name. Type records in the editor, one per line: app.practice.test. 300 IN A 192.0.2.42. The final dot makes the name absolute; 300 is its lifetime in seconds (caching is not simulated). IN means Internet class."
          },
          {
            "title": "Save, publish, inspect",
            "text": "Save zone changes only the saved copy. Reload primary publishes it. A secondary keeps its prior copy until an update sees a newer SOA serial, the zone revision number. Look up reads the selected served copy, not unsaved editor text."
          },
          {
            "title": "Mail and nameservers",
            "text": "NS names a zone nameserver. MX names a mail host and preference; smaller preference is preferred. Each in-zone target needs its own A or AAAA address. DNS records alone do not prove the service is reachable."
          }
        ],
